Experiment – 10

Design and implement a DNS and DDNS simulation to illustrate how domain names are resolved and how records can be updated dynamically. Build a simple client-server system capable of handling DNS queries and updates.

What is DNS?
The Domain Name System (DNS) is a mechanism that maps domain names to their corresponding IP addresses, allowing devices to communicate over a network.

Steps:-
Implement a DNS server using sockets to store mappings between domain names and IP addresses.
Run the server so it continuously listens for requests from clients.
Create a client application that can connect to the DNS server.
The client sends a QUERY request to find the IP address of a given domain.
The server searches its records and returns the appropriate IP address.
The client can also send an UPDATE request to insert or modify DNS entries.
The server updates its internal data structure with the new information.
The client displays the response received from the server.
Perform multiple queries and updates to demonstrate dynamic DNS behavior.

Practical Steps:-
Configure PC0 with IP address → 192.168.1.1
Configure the DNS Server with IP → 192.168.1.
Configure the Web Server with IP → 192.168.1.3
On PC0, set the DNS server address to 192.168.1.2
Open the DNS Server, go to Services → DNS, and enable the service
Create a DNS entry:
Name: www.krmu.edu
Address: 192.168.1.200
On the Web Server, enable HTTP service from Services → HTTP
On PC0, open Desktop → Web Browser and enter the URL: www.krmu.edu
The DNS server resolves the domain name to its IP, establishes a connection to the web server, and the webpage is successfully displayed.


